  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14094731/assets/6715fe4c0f0b69044ee93da6d138a8d79cc9b509e7c912f79467d9f0b8e6d6be.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence $B$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14094731/assets/4464c66c00bc020668bef5e346caee352228403068be1334fc4997439140ae7f.png)",
